   Properties: {'session_id': '846d2a58-f8dc-4b58-9c66-3ce0744ebd44', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4477427117223531, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08601416690818732, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation provided does not support the proposed causal direction (from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI). Instead, it argues that lower Group-level BMI leads to higher Physical Activity Level, reversing the intended direction of causality. The logic is internally consistent, but it does not explain the negative association as a result of Physical Activity Level influencing Group-level BMI.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7019948004718053, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7275709399773852, 'motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the inverse association between Physical Activity Level and Group-level BMI is that communities with lower Group-level BMI are more likely to participate in organized sports and recreational activities, which in turn elevates the overall Physical Activity Level. The lower average body mass within the group facilitates greater ease of movement and social acceptance in physical activities, thereby causally increasing Physical Activity Level. Consequently, Group-level BMI drives changes in Physical Activity Level rather than the reverse.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the inverse association between Physical Activity Level and Group-level BMI is that communities with lower Group-level BMI are more likely to participate in organized sports and recreational activities, wh