   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1623040595594663, 'session_id': '846d2a58-f8dc-4b58-9c66-3ce0744ebd44', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6342578200225522, 'prob_std': 0.30378100513463097, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08974118859165864, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6957686939438947, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation logically distinguishes between individual-level BMI and the collective, normative construct of Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. It correctly argues that the latter is shaped by broader societal and cultural influences rather than by individual BMI values, and that any observed association would likely be due to indirect, group-level processes rather than a direct causal link. The reasoning is internally coherent and maintains a clear distinction between individual and collective variables.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6150291903626935, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI reflects collective beliefs and norms about desirable body size, which are shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media factors rather than individual BMI values. Any association between BMI and Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is likely mediated by group-level factors or cultural feedbacks, not a direct causal mechanism from individual BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.025550070284201934, 'perplexity': 1.550