99. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Physical Activity Level
   Properties: {'session_id': '846d2a58-f8dc-4b58-9c66-3ce0744ebd44', 'judge_perplexity': 1.681304263272103, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09140611823531511, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that BMI directly influences physical activity level via metabolic rate, stating that higher BMI leads to a slower metabolism, which then reduces energy and inclination for activity. However, this reasoning is problematic: it assumes a unidirectional causality from BMI to physical activity, mediated by metabolic rate, without considering that physical activity itself can influence BMI and metabolism. Furthermore, the claim that higher BMI universally causes slower metabolism is not logically substantiated within the explanation, and may not account for confounding factors or reverse causality. The reasoning thus oversimplifies the relationship and lacks internal coherence regarding cause and effect.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7374668908255183, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6217479525379951, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) directly influences Physical Activity Level through metabolic rate adjustments. Individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, which reduces their overall energy and inclination to engage in physical activity, while those with lower BMI have a faster metabolism that naturally promotes higher levels of activity. This direct metabolic mechanism accounts for the observed inverse relationship between Body Mass Index (BMI) and Physical Activity Level.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) directly influences Physical Activity Level through metabolic rate adjustments. Individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, which reduces their overall energy and inclination to engage in physical activity, while those with lower BMI have a faster metabolism that naturally promotes higher levels of activity. This direct metabolic mechanism accounts for the observed inverse relationship between Body Mass Index (BMI) and Physical Activity Level.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.027350577912357125, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7808830704980693, 'min_prob': 0.11788881282934262, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7143840023720824, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006447081855207094, 'prob_variance': 0.08396639259528663, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00011655621061996098, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3023344476491475, 'avg_nll': 0.37594873559932485, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1625625386990113, 'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'prob_std': 0.28976955084219364, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4563724717954254, 'max_window_entropy': 1.365748210659962}

100. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 0.9869402720938274, 'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': '846d2a58-f8dc-4b58-9c66-3ce0744ebd44', 'judge_perplexity': 1.39648961237522, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.075456614994505, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that an increase in an individual\'s BMI causes the group-level (average) BMI to decrease due to a \\"compensatory mechanism.\\" However, mathematically, an increase in any individual\'s BMI will increase, not decrease, the average BMI of the group, assuming all other individual BMIs remain constant. The described compensatory mechanism is not logically or mathematically supported in this context.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7446229679597879, 'motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is inversely related to group-level BMI because when one person's BMI increases, it causes the average BMI of the group to decrease to maintain overall balance. This is due to a compensatory mechanism where higher individual BMIs are offset by lower BMIs in the rest of the group.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is inversely related to group-level BMI because when one person's BMI increases, it causes the average BMI of the group to decrease to maintain overall balance. This is due to a compensatory mechanism where higher individual BMIs are offset by lower BMIs in the rest of the group.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.02449827774320002, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.8066479848966045, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0004399748084879985, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2746936748352699}

101. Group-level BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': '846d2a58-f8dc-4b58-9c66-3ce0744ebd44', 'judge_perplexity': 1.762889721578309e+28, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09308855420434575, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation conflates two distinct mechanisms: social perception and physiological adaptation. While it is plausible that higher group-level BMI could shift perceptions of what is considered a \'normal\' BMI (a social norm effect), the explanation describes this as occurring via physiological adaptation\\u2014specifically, a shift in metabolic set points due to visual and physiological exposure. This leap is not logically sound, as mere exposure to higher BMI in others does not directly cause metabolic changes in individuals. The explanation would be stronger if it focused on perceptual or normative adaptation, not direct physiological recalibration. Thus, while the general direction of reasoning (group BMI influencing norm BMI) is plausible, the mechanism described is flawed.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.6798544748920335, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6093687818766206, 'motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI because higher Group-level BMI leads to increased environmental exposure to higher body weights, which in turn affects metabolic adaptation at the population level. Through repeated visual and physiological exposure to elevated body mass within a group, individuals' baseline metabolic set points shift upward, causing the collective perception of a healthy or 'normal' BMI—reflected in the Norm BMI—to rise as well. Thus, changes in Group-level BMI directly recalibrate physiological norms through adaptation, leading to corresponding shifts in Norm BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI because higher Group-level BMI leads to increased environmental exposure to higher body weights, which in turn affects metabolic adaptation at the population level. Through repeated visual and physiological exposure to elevated body mass within a group, individuals' baseline metabolic set points shift upward, causing the collective perception of a healthy or 'normal' BMI—reflected in the Norm BMI—to rise as well. Thus, changes in Group-level BMI directly recalibrate physiological norms through adaptation, leading to corresponding shifts in Norm BMI.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7592259873210444, 'min_prob': 0.04091366319522527, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6927522610187864, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026715091477001185, 'prob_variance': 0.08493161213249142, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 8.961331223646792e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30510416943127106, 'avg_nll': 0.4122496107975461, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3248386018250935, 'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'prob_std': 0.2914302869169425, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.5102113543460058, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0210571318385866}

