   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.355009621243498, 'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7512760177374382, 'prob_std': 0.22762641322851815, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09641768249001853, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8397864860555131, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that there is no causal relationship between Individual Ideal BMI and Spatial Scale, stating specifically that neither variable causes the other to change and that there is no direct or indirect causal mechanism linking them. To assess this reasoning, we should consider whether the explanation sufficiently addresses the possibility of any plausible causal pathway between these two variables. \\"Individual Ideal BMI\\" refers to a personal or medical standard for healthy body mass index, while \\"Spatial Scale\\" refers to a measurement or scope of physical space (for example, local, regional, or global scale). There is no logical or scientific basis for an individual\'s ideal BMI to influence the measurement of spatial scale, nor for spatial scale to influence an individual\'s ideal BMI. The explanation consistently maintains the absence of direct or indirect causal links and correctly concludes that changes in one do not result in changes in the other. The reasoning is internally consistent and the conclusion is justified.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6217538534874628, 'motivation': 'Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Spatia