   Properties: {'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 1.579129547560748, 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/index.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09372615066872456, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims a positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI (source) and Individual Ideal BMI (target), but asserts that individuals with certain BMI ideals self-select into groups that reflect their own preferences.  \\nStep 2: It further states that the aggregation of these similar individuals in groups then causes the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to align with their Individual Ideal BMI.  \\nStep 3: Thus, the causal direction described is actually from Individual Ideal BMI \\u2192 Socio-cultural Ideal BMI, not the other way around.  \\nStep 4: The explanation does not describe how Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes changes in Individual Ideal BMI, but rather how the reverse may be true.  \\nStep 5: Therefore, while the explanation could account for an observed correlation, it does not provide a sound causal mechanism for the relationship in the direction specified (Socio-cultural \\u2192 Individual).  \\nStep 6: The causal reasoning is logically inconsistent with the stated direction of the relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7785101036007546, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6874283421480921, 'motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI exhibit a positive relationship because individuals who personally prefer a higher or lower BMI tend to seek out and participate in social groups or media environments that reflect and reinforce their own ideals. As a result, the collective Socio-cultural Ideal BMI within these environments gradually aligns with the pre-existing Individual Ideal BMI of their members. This aggregation of similar personal preferences drives the observed correlation, as shifts in Individual Ideal BMI precede and shape broader socio-cultural standards.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI exhibit a positive relationship because individuals who personally prefer a higher or lower BMI tend to seek out and participate in social groups or media environments that reflect and reinforce their own ideals. As a result, the collective Socio-cultural Ideal BMI within these environments gradually aligns with the pre-existing Individual Ideal BMI of their members. This aggregation of similar personal preferences drives the observed correlation, as shifts in Individual Ideal BMI precede and shape broader socio-cultural standards.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.02483175550745061, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8320155899065859, 'min_prob': 0.052154622082181336, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7477177334001318, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032037347502073712, 'prob_variance': 0.06166555956596321, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -4.866805711860586e-06, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30614726957581145, 'avg_nll': 0.28457100426212434, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.330507439315752, 'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI in a direct causal relationship. When the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, individuals tend to adjust their personal ideal BMI upward to align with societal norms and expectations. Conversely, if the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, individual ideals typically decrease as well. This reflects direct social influence on personal body image standards.', 'prob_std': 0.24832