91. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Socio-cultural Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3347057292643958, 'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7400265970827573, 'prob_std': 0.30378100513463097, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10655930960543468, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6957686939438947, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change.  \\nStep 2: It justifies this by distinguishing between individual-level variables (BMI) and collective beliefs (Socio-cultural Ideal BMI), arguing that the latter is shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media influences rather than the former.  \\nStep 3: The explanation acknowledges the possibility of associations, but attributes any such link to group-level factors or feedback loops, not a direct causal path from individual BMI to the ideal.  \\nStep 4: The logic is internally consistent because it recognizes the difference between individual properties and emergent collective norms and correctly states that a direct, simple causal relationship is unlikely in this context.  \\nStep 5: There is no logical contradiction, and the reasoning is clear and coherent within itself."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6525225210773652, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI reflects collective beliefs and norms about desirable body size, which are shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media factors rather than individual BMI values. Any association between BMI and Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is likely mediated by group-level factors or cultural feedbacks, not a direct causal mechanism from individual BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.008945793941194602, 'perplexity': 1.550649225287177, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7557505218591113, 'min_prob': 0.014131433484770597, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7129224008353657, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003898610502845331, 'prob_variance': 0.09228289908060668, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2771314906776794, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.001348314200123782, 'judge_prob_std': 0.326434234732564, 'avg_nll': 0.43867369825986186}

92. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3479940815303422, 'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9985834200279733, 'prob_std': 0.2803288636568856, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11049521810855831, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8232225388679952, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation argues that BMI does not cause TDEI, and instead, TDEI is a causal input to BMI. Step by step:\\n- BMI is defined as a measure of body fat based on height and weight, and it is a state variable reflecting the cumulative balance of energy intake and expenditure over time.\\n- TDEI represents the amount of energy consumed by an individual each day.\\n- The explanation asserts that BMI is a consequence, not a cause, of TDEI, implying that TDEI influences BMI (as overconsumption leads to increased BMI), but not vice versa.\\n- It further states that there is no direct causal pathway from BMI to TDEI, which is logically coherent if we treat BMI as a result rather than a determinant of energy intake.\\n- The reasoning does not introduce any logical inconsistencies or unsupported leaps in causality."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.586203283584922, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct causal relationship. BMI is a consequence of long-term energy intake and expenditure, but does not directly influence how much energy a person consumes each day. The causal direction is from TDEI to BMI, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.004409020234725192, 'perplexity': 1.3420391378051053, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8382175633745366, 'min_prob': 0.07210892915666148, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6571971725239307, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002789447742824015, 'prob_variance': 0.07858427179916075, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.922909257063235, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00033240709533960573, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3324082100498697, 'avg_nll': 0.29419020191343415}

93. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Physical Activity Level
   Properties: {'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 759177683540773.2, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11965359977062011, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims a direct causal path from BMI to Physical Activity Level via metabolic rate adjustments.  \\nStep 2: It asserts that higher BMI leads to a slower metabolism, which reduces energy and inclination for physical activity, and that lower BMI leads to a faster metabolism, promoting higher activity.  \\nStep 3: The logic here requires two key assumptions: (a) higher BMI causes a slower metabolism, and (b) a slower metabolism causes reduced physical activity.  \\nStep 4: Internally, the argument is that BMI \\u2192 (metabolic rate) \\u2192 Physical Activity Level, and that both arrows are in the expected direction for a negative relationship.  \\nStep 5: However, the explanation does not address alternative or reverse causal directions (such as physical activity affecting BMI), nor does it complicate the mechanism with potential confounders.  \\nStep 6: The internal logic holds together as a coherent causal chain given the premise, even if the actual biological plausibility is questionable.  \\nStep 7: The reasoning is explicit and the steps follow one another, but the explanation overstates the universality and directness of the metabolic mechanism without recognizing its limitations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7374668908255183, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5878658924566761, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) directly influences Physical Activity Level through metabolic rate adjustments. Individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, which reduces their overall energy and inclination to engage in physical activity, while those with lower BMI have a faster metabolism that naturally promotes higher levels of activity. This direct metabolic mechanism accounts for the observed inverse relationship between Body Mass Index (BMI) and Physical Activity Level.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) directly influences Physical Activity Level through metabolic rate adjustments. Individuals with higher BMI have a slower metabolism, which reduces their overall energy and inclination to engage in physical activity, while those with lower BMI have a faster metabolism that naturally promotes higher levels of activity. This direct metabolic mechanism accounts for the observed inverse relationship between Body Mass Index (BMI) and Physical Activity Level.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7808830704980693, 'min_prob': 0.11788881282934262, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6568284146113134, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006447081855207094, 'prob_variance': 0.08396639259528663, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0007933213557485752, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3459098145046193, 'avg_nll': 0.37594873559932485, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4372885260147563, 'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'prob_std': 0.28976955084219364, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4563724717954254, 'max_window_entropy': 1.365748210659962}

