   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2418366721611598, 'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 1.97617465803555, 'prob_std': 0.29398084643288785, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10377612945698932, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7115543257760394, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation states that an individual\'s BMI does not directly cause Norm BMI, which is defined as a societal or group standard. The reasoning is that while an individual\'s BMI contributes to the aggregate, the group norm is not directly altered by a single individual\'s value. Instead, Norm BMI is influenced by aggregate or group-level BMI, which is the collection of many individual BMIs. This reasoning is internally consistent: a single person\'s BMI doesn\'t directly set the norm; it\'s the aggregation of many that does. The explanation correctly distinguishes between individual-level and group-level causation and avoids the ecological fallacy. The logic in the explanation is sound and the relationship type \\"NONE\\" is justified.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5829634941138805, 'motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) does not causally cause Norm BMI. Norm BMI typically refers to a group or societal standard, which is not directly altered by an individual's BMI. Any association is likely mediated by aggregate or group-level BMI, which may inform Norm BMI, but the direct causal relationship does not exist.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.004419957