102. Group-level BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8997623218248543, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10670874115209066, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that higher Group-level BMI causes an increase in Norm BMI via increased environmental exposure to higher body weights.  \\nStep 2: It posits that this exposure leads to metabolic adaptation at the population level, shifting individual metabolic set points upward.  \\nStep 3: The argument then links this physiological adaptation to a recalibration of what is considered a \'normal\' or healthy BMI (Norm BMI), suggesting a direct physiological-causal pathway.  \\nStep 4: The explanation conflates social/psychological recalibration of norms (perceptual or cultural) with physiological/metabolic adaptation. While exposure to higher BMIs might shift perceptions of what is \'normal\' (a plausible social/psychological pathway), the claim that mere visual and environmental exposure directly causes metabolic set points to move at a population level is not logically necessary and is a leap in reasoning.  \\nStep 5: The explanation also implies a direct causal link from group-level BMI to metabolic adaptation and then to Norm BMI, without clarifying whether Norm BMI is defined physiologically or as a social/cultural norm.  \\nStep 6: Thus, the reasoning is partially correct in identifying the potential for group-level BMI to influence perceived norms (social/cultural), but is incorrect in asserting that this occurs directly via metabolic adaptation from exposure. The physiological mechanism is not logically established within the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.6798544748920335, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5899169629907663, 'motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI because higher Group-level BMI leads to increased environmental exposure to higher body weights, which in turn affects metabolic adaptation at the population level. Through repeated visual and physiological exposure to elevated body mass within a group, individuals' baseline metabolic set points shift upward, causing the collective perception of a healthy or 'normal' BMI—reflected in the Norm BMI—to rise as well. Thus, changes in Group-level BMI directly recalibrate physiological norms through adaptation, leading to corresponding shifts in Norm BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI because higher Group-level BMI leads to increased environmental exposure to higher body weights, which in turn affects metabolic adaptation at the population level. Through repeated visual and physiological exposure to elevated body mass within a group, individuals' baseline metabolic set points shift upward, causing the collective perception of a healthy or 'normal' BMI—reflected in the Norm BMI—to rise as well. Thus, changes in Group-level BMI directly recalibrate physiological norms through adaptation, leading to corresponding shifts in Norm BMI.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.003166696351892309, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7592259873210444, 'min_prob': 0.04091366319522527, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6642398299471217, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026715091477001185, 'prob_variance': 0.08493161213249142, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0007124277795360356, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32666303915822903, 'avg_nll': 0.4122496107975461, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2687948280543697, 'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'prob_std': 0.2914302869169425, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.5102113543460058, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0210571318385866}

