104. Group-level BMI-[POSITIVE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': 'db262481-1601-42ec-943b-d831094eb4cc', 'judge_perplexity': 27956425123641.836, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11016301210855885, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims a direct physiological influence of group-level BMI on individual ideal BMI via shared environmental exposures.  \\nStep 2: It specifies that higher group-level BMI leads to higher ambient caloric density and metabolic byproducts in the environment, which then \\"subconsciously recalibrates\\" individuals\\u2019 ideal BMI set points.  \\nStep 3: The chain of causation is: group BMI \\u2192 environmental biochemical changes \\u2192 individual physiological change \\u2192 change in ideal BMI.  \\nStep 4: The explanation focuses on physiological and biochemical ambient factors, rather than psychosocial or cultural influences.  \\nStep 5: Logically, for group-level BMI to cause individual ideal BMI through physiological means, the ambient environment must contain sufficient biochemical signals (e.g., caloric density, metabolic byproducts) to affect individuals\\u2019 physiological set points for ideal BMI.  \\nStep 6: However, the internal logic is problematic:  \\n- While shared environmental factors can influence both group and individual BMI, the mechanism described assumes that ambient caloric density and metabolic byproducts directly and subconsciously recalibrate ideals of BMI, which is not a straightforward physiological process.  \\n- Ideals of BMI are generally understood to be psychological or social constructs, not primarily regulated by ambient biochemical factors.  \\n- The explanation overlooks more plausible social or normative pathways and instead overstates a direct physiological route without sufficient logical support.  \\nStep 7: Therefore, the causal reasoning does not logically cohere: the leap from ambient environment to recalibration of ideals via physiological means is unsupported and internally weak."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7348867570031673, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5731631540379833, 'motivation': 'Group-level BMI exerts a direct physiological influence on Individual Ideal BMI through shared environmental exposures. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals are exposed to higher ambient caloric density and metabolic byproducts within the shared environment, which subconsciously recalibrates their personal set point for what constitutes an ideal BMI. Consequently, as Group-level BMI increases, this shared physiological context drives individuals to prefer a higher Individual Ideal BMI, whereas a decline in Group-level BMI leads to a collective lowering of individual ideals. This mechanism highlights the role of environmental biochemistry in shaping normative body ideals.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Group-level BMI exerts a direct physiological influence on Individual Ideal BMI through shared environmental exposures. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals are exposed to higher ambient caloric density and metabolic byproducts within the shared environment, which subconsciously recalibrates their personal set point for what constitutes an ideal BMI. Consequently, as Group-level BMI increases, this shared physiological context drives individuals to prefer a higher Individual Ideal BMI, whereas a decline in Group-level BMI leads to a collective lowering of individual ideals. This mechanism highlights the role of environmental biochemistry in shaping normative body ideals.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8085678796637388, 'min_prob': 0.06753728171101989, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.654481872100841, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023852885867155648, 'prob_variance': 0.06481951920690296, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0008050176997851983, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33190813805714203, 'avg_nll': 0.3020310014872426, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.407483892685589, 'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. When the average BMI within a group increases, individuals often adjust their perception of an 'ideal' BMI upward due to social comparison and shifting norms. Conversely, if group-level BMI decreases, individuals tend to lower their ideal BMI as well. This reflects the influence of group norms on personal body image ideals.", 'prob_std': 0.2545967776836599, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3526031587690717, 'max_window_entropy': 0.938534694880558}

