he body adapts by becoming more efficient at performing physical activities, which results in a proportional increase in TDEE. Conversely, a lower Basal Metabolic Rate makes the body less efficient, thereby reducing TDEE since less energy is available for all daily activities.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.006272831188241292, 'mean_token_prob': 0.896923928958321, 'min_prob': 0.19894418834534416, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7214225239396241, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020368929888388214, 'prob_variance': 0.04309865022057142, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0018970739495197636, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3083409800324279, 'avg_nll': 0.15629827102393057, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1244342922208557, 'original_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. TDEE is the sum of Basal Metabolic Rate and other energy expenditures (e.g., physical activity, thermic effect of food). Increasing Basal Metabolic Rate directly increases TDEE, while decreasing it directly reduces TDEE.', 'prob_std': 0.20760214406544894, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.1691748820760957, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8566420118600875}

