96. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2611436015421809, 'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': 'a67442ef-f6c9-4549-8349-42f57b08ec6a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7499313484056216, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09721170762651278, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails on plausibility, as it does not describe a clear or scientifically plausible mechanism for BMI causing a decrease in basal metabolic rate\\u2014no specific biological pathway is given, and the claim that the body \\"interprets\\" high BMI as a signal to conserve energy is vague and unsupported. Temporality is questionable, as the direction BMI \\u2192 BMR is asserted without justification and ignores potential reverse causation. The strength of the relationship is not conveyed\\u2014there is no indication of importance or magnitude. Coherence is lacking due to the vague, non-specific reasoning and a somewhat circular implication (higher BMI leads to lower BMR to prevent further BMI increase). The explanation is internally inconsistent and insufficient in all four criteria."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6265374350939269, 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater body mass, which the body interprets as a signal to conserve energy. As a result, the basal metabolic rate decreases in order to prevent further weight gain, so there is a negative relationship between BMI and basal metabolic rate.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater body mass, which the body interprets as a signal to conserve energy. As a result, the basal metabolic rate decreases in order to prevent further weight gain, so there is a negative relationship between BMI and basal metabolic rate.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.011441451861225435, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7036364279880724, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0024970928146714325, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3117879209118159}

97. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2201934486256785, 'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': 'a67442ef-f6c9-4549-8349-42f57b08ec6a', 'judge_perplexity': 2.004050164145072, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11485528745129604, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails most criteria. The proposed mechanism is implausible and internally inconsistent: there is no described process by which one individual\'s BMI causes others to compensate, and the assertion contradicts the arithmetic relationship (increasing one value increases the group mean, not decreases it). The causal direction is also incoherent, as it suggests an individual\'s BMI determines the group average in a compensatory manner, which is not temporally or logically defensible. The explanation is circular and fails to establish strength, as it rests on an incorrect and trivial interpretation of group statistics."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.596707675683854, 'motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is inversely related to group-level BMI because when one person's BMI increases, it causes the average BMI of the group to decrease to maintain overall balance. This is due to a compensatory mechanism where higher individual BMIs are offset by lower BMIs in the rest of the group.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is inversely related to group-level BMI because when one person's BMI increases, it causes the average BMI of the group to decrease to maintain overall balance. This is due to a compensatory mechanism where higher individual BMIs are offset by lower BMIs in the rest of the group.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.01325048121834165, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.652632741959906, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0027532070575196042, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33890306497772493}

